= Issues of floating point math <sec:float>

- common problems: *overflow* and *round-off errors*
- *two's complement*: _most significant bit_ (MSB) (a.k.a. sign bit $s$) flags negative numbers
- floating point representations have a *base* $beta$ ($beta = 2$ in IEEE standard), *precision* $p$ (a.k.a _mantissa_), and *exponent* $e$
  - representation not unique, shifting exponent _and_ mantissa can give same number multiple times; solution: _normalization_, i.e. leading digit before the point always 1
  - number in exponent $E$ stored with _bias_ $b$ (hence $E$ always positive as opposed to two's complement), e.g. $e = E - 127$ for floats
- in general, value $f$ of IEEE float given by $f = (-1)^s (1 + M/2^p) dot 2^(E - b)$
- *machine precision*: smallest increment in mantissa, $eps_m = 1/2^p$
  - pitfall: $a + b = a$ even for $b != 0$ if $|b| < eps_m |a|$

= Integration of ordinary differential equations

- we seek solution $by(t)$ to eq. of the form $(dif by)/(dif t) = bf(by, t)$ subject to boundary condition, most commonly _initial value problem_ (IVP)
- forward or *explicit Euler method*: $by_(n+1) = by_n + bf(by_n) Delta t + cO_s (Delta t^2)$
  - simplest approach, but only first-order accurate $cO_s (Delta t^2)$ and unstable for large stepsize $Delta t$
  - explicit because r.h.s. depends only on known quantities
- backward or *implicit Euler method*: $by_(n+1) = by_n + bf(by_(n+1)) Delta t$
  - excellent stability, hence useful for _stiff eqs._, but still inaccurate for large $Delta t$
  - often involves root-finding of nonlinear eq., numerically expensive
- *implicit midpoint rule*: $by_(n+1) = by_n + bf((by_n + by_(n+1))/2) Delta t + cO_s (Delta t^3)$
  - second-order accurate but implicit so difficult in practice
- *Runge-Kutta methods*: derive from exact solution $by_(n+1) = by_n + integral_(t_n)^(t_(n+1)) bf(by(t)) dif t$ by approximating integral with trapezoidal rule:
  $
    bk_1 = bf(by_n, t_n), quad bk_2 = bf(by_n + bk_1 Delta t, t_(n+1)), \
    by_(n+1) = by_n + 1/2 (bk_1 + bk_2) Delta t + cO_s (Delta t^3)
  $
  - a.k.a. predictor-corrector scheme, $bk_1$ = predictor, $bk_2$ = corrector
  - 2nd-order accurate, higher-order schemes exist, e.g. RK-4:
  $
    bk_1 & = bf(by_n, t_n), quad bk_2 = bf(by_n + bk_1 (Delta t)/2, t_n + (Delta t)/2), \
    bk_3 & = bf(by_n + bk_2 (Delta t)/2, t_n + (Delta t)/2), bk_4 = bf(by_n + bk_3 Delta t, t_n + Delta t), \
    by_(n+1) & = by_n + 1/6 (bk_1 + 2 bk_2 + 2 bk_3 + bk_4) Delta t + cO_s (Delta t^5)
  $
- *adaptive step size*: achieve optimum compromise between accuracy, efficiency and stability by e.g. estimating error based on difference between one full step and two half-steps, define local error bounds, increase stepsize if below, decrease if above
- *reduction to 1st order*: bring higher-order diff. eqs. into standard form, e.g. cast $dot.double(bx) = bf(x)$ into $dot(by) = tilde(bf)(by)$ by defining $by = (bx, dot(bx))$ and $tilde(bf) = (dot(bx), bf(bx))$, then apply e.g. Runge-Kutta
- *leapfrog*: very simple alternative in above case; define $bv equiv dot(bx)$, then
  $ bx_(n+1) = bx_n + bv_(n + 1/2) Delta t, quad bv_(n + 3/2) = bv_(n + 1/2) + bf(bx_(n+1)) Delta t $
  - 2nd-order accurate and requires only one evaluation per step
  - name leapfrog due to interleaved advances of position and velocity
  - better than higher-order schemes on some systems due to being symplectic and time-reversible
- *symplectic integrator*: structure-preserving method that observes special properties of Hamiltonian systems, e.g. energy and phase-space volume conservation (_Liouville theorem_)
  - let $omega : RR^d times RR^d -> RR$, $omega(xi, eta) = A_(P(xi times eta))$ give area of parallelogram $P(xi times eta)$ spanned by $xi, eta in RR^d$; then linear map $F : RR^d -> RR^d$ is symplectic if $omega(F xi, F eta) = omega(xi, eta)$
  - a differentiable map $g : U -> RR^d$ is symplectic if its Jacobian is symplectic everywhere
  - *Poincaré's theorem*: time evolution generated by a Hamiltonian in phase-space is a symplectic transformation

= Collisionless particle systems <sec:collisionless>

- central idea: describe large-$N$-body system as small-$N$ system by grouping real particles into fiducial macro particles
  - for gravity, the e.o.m.s with (numerical) _softening length_ $eps$ are $dot.double(bx)_i = -vb(nabla) Phi(bx_i)$, $Phi(bx) = -G sum_(j=1)^N m_j/sqrt((bx - bx_j)^2 + eps^2)$
  - purpose of $eps$: prevent (highly correlated) bound states (requires $ev(v^2) >> (G m)/eps$), avoid numerical expense of large angle scattering in singular potential; $eps$ introduces smallest resolved length scale
- examples: electrons in plasma, globular star clusters, stars in a galaxy, dark matter (all are also _uncorrelated_ to good approx.)

= Tree algorithms

- more spec. *hierarchical multipole algorithms* enable fast, approximate force calculations for $N$-body systems (see @sec:collisionless)
  - group hierarchy organized in _tree-like_ data structure
  - scale with $cO(N ln N)$, whereas _direct summation_ is $cO(N^2)$
- central idea: use multipole expansion for (grav.) potential created by group of distant particles rather than sum up forces individually
  - _monopole_: $M = sum_(i=1)^N m_i$, _quadrupole_: $Q_(i j) = sum_(k=1)^N m_k [3 (bs - bx_k)_i (bs - bx_k)_j - delta_(i j) (bs - bx_k)^2]$, where $bs$ points to c.o.m.
  - dipole vanishes when expansion is done relative to c.o.m.
  - expansion accurate if $r/d <= theta$ for chosen *opening angle* $theta$; $r$, $d$ radius and distance of particle group

= The particle-mesh technique

- alternative to tree algorithms, used in MD and astrophysics
- 1. construct density field $rho$ on suitable auxiliary mesh 2. compute potential $Phi$ on mesh by solving Poisson's eq. 3. calculate force field $bF$ from potential 4. calculate forces $bF_i$ at original particle positions
  + mass assignment onto auxiliary mesh via _nearest grid point_ (NGP), _clouds in cell_ (CIC), or _triangular shaped clouds_ (TSC)
    - NGP: density jumps discont. when particle crosses cell boundary, resulting force piece-wise constant; CIC: force piece-wise linear and continuous, but derivative jumps; TSC: both force and its derivative smooth
  + solve Poisson's eq. $Delta Phi = 4 pi G rho$ either with Fourier transform (@sec:fourier) or iteratively (@sec:iterative)
  + calculate acceleration $ba = -vb(nabla) Phi$ with *finite differencing*, e.g. $a_x (i, j, k) = -1/(2 h) [Phi(i + 1, j, k) - Phi(i - 1, j, k)] + cO(h^2)$ or with _larger stencil_ to reduce truncation error below $cO(h^2)$
  + interpolate from mesh to particles: $bF_i (bx_i) = m sum_vb(p) ba_vb(p) W(bx_i - bx_vb(p))$, where sum runs over auxiliary mesh cells $vb(p) = (i, j, k)$
    - $W$ _same_ assignment kernel as used in density construction, ensures vanishing _self-force_ and pairwise antisymmetric forces

= Force calculation with Fourier transform techniques <sec:fourier>

- tool for solving certain PDEs, e.g. Poisson's eq. $Delta Phi = 4 pi G rho$; sol. $Phi(bx) = -G integral (rho(bx') dif bx')/(|bx - bx'|) = rho star g$ is convolution of $rho$ with $g(bx) = -G/(|bx|)$
- *convolution thm.*: $cF(f star g) = cF(f) dot cF(g) => Phi = cF^(-1) [cF(rho) dot cF(g)]$
- in practice, to solve this requires assuming _periodic_ b.c. in box of size $L$, gives finite discrete $bk$-space so we can apply (_discrete_) _fast Fourier transform_ (FFT), scales with $cO(N ln N)$
- for nonperiodic problems: *zero-padding* constructs mesh s.t. sources lives only in one quarter (in 2D), rest of mesh is zeroed-out

= Iterative solvers and the multigrid technique <sec:iterative>

- ansatz: discretize $Phi$ on regular $N$-point mesh with spacing $h$ so Poisson's eq. at cell $i in {0, ..., N - 1}$ is $(Phi_(i+1) - 2 Phi_i + Phi_(i-1))/h^2 = 4 pi G rho_i$
- above can be cast into set of $N$ linear eqs. $bA bPhi = bb$ with $bA$ stencil matrix, $bb = 4 pi G (rho_0, ..., rho_(N-1))$; analytic. solvable with Gauss elimination, but not feasible for large $N$ (scales $cO(N^3)$)
- use *Jacobi* or *Gauss-Seidel iteration* instead by decomposing $bA = bD - upright(bold(U)) - bL$, yields Jac. update formula $Phi_i^((n+1)) = 1/2 (Phi_(i+1)^((n)) + Phi_(i-1)^((n)) - 4 pi G h^2 rho_i)$ or GS $bPhi^((n+1)) = bD^(-1) [upright(bold(U)) bPhi^((n)) + bL bPhi^((n+1)) + bb]$
  - GS looks like, but is not implicit formula; no values of $bPhi^((n+1))$ are needed, because $bL$ has only elements below diagonal
  - GS makes use of updated values immediately, $approx$ twice as fast as Jacobi, but has to be solved sequentially $=>$ not parallelizable
- *red-black ordering*: some update rules allow decomposition of cells into disjoint sets whose update depends only on cells from other set(s), e.g. for Poisson's eq.: chessboard pattern
- convergence of Jac. and GS often '_stalls_'; high-$nu$ errors die quickly, but low-$nu$, high-$lambda$ errors slowly; reason: per step, information travels only one stencil length, convergence needs several sweeps
- solution: *multigrid*; central idea: go to coarser mesh, compute improved initial guess, helps speed up convergence on fine grid
  - because multigrid operates on coarser grid for most of time, it is _much_ faster, cheaper than Jac. and GS
  - fine $->$ coarse and back requires restriction and prolongation operators; in 1D: restr. with $upright(bold(I))_h^(2 h) = [1/4, 1/2, 1/4]$, prol. with $upright(bold(I))_(2 h)^h = ]1/2, 1, 1/2[$

= Molecular dynamics simulations

- aim: simulate atoms, molecules, proteins, etc. in _microscopic_ detail over relevant timespan to determine mech., electr., etc. properties
- important point: uses Newton's _classical_ e.o.m.s, based on approximation of inter- and/or intra-molecular forces, force laws may be empirical or quantum mechanically derived
- intimately connected to stat. mech.: precise microstate unimportant, interested in _ensemble averages_ of macro. var. $T$, $P$, $rho$, etc.
- *ergodic hypothesis*: postulates ensemble avr. = time avr., thus evolve MD sim. to reach equilibrium, then measure time avr. $overline(A) = lim_(tau -> infinity) 1/tau integral_(t_0)^(t_0 + tau) A(Gamma(t)) dif t$ of macros. quantity $A(Gamma)$, fct. of microstate $Gamma$
- *finite range interactions*: molecules interact only over short range, e.g. _Lennard-Jones potential_ $V(r) = 4 eps [sigma^12/r^12 - sigma^6/r^6] =>$ introduce cutoff $V_c (r) = 0$ for $r > r_c$ and search grid, ignore particles in cells farther than $r_c$; reduces scaling from $cO(N^2)$ to $cO(N)$

= Basic gas dynamics

- *Euler eqs.*: describe continuity of mass, momentum, energy during dynamics of ideal, i.e. frictionless, gas
  $ (partial rho)/(partial t) + vb(nabla) dot (rho bv) = 0, (partial rho bv)/(partial t) + vb(nabla) (rho bv^2 + P) = 0, (partial rho e)/(partial t) + vb(nabla) dot [(rho e + P) bv] = 0 $
  with total energy (per unit mass) $e = u + bv^2/2$, $u$ thermal energy; closed by eq. of state $P = (gamma - 1) rho u$ with adiabatic index $gamma = C_P \/ C_V$
- *Navier-Stokes eqs.*: describe dynamics of real fluids with internal stress due to _viscosity_ (dissipates relative motion into heat)
  $ (partial rho bv)/(partial t) + vb(nabla) (rho bv^2 + P) = vb(nabla) bPi, quad (partial rho e)/(partial t) + vb(nabla) dot [(rho e + P) bv] = vb(nabla) (bPi bv) $
  (continuity eq. identical to Euler) with viscous stress tensor (material property) $bPi = eta [vb(nabla) bv + (vb(nabla) bv)^upright(T) - 2/3 (vb(nabla) dot bv) one] + xi (vb(nabla) dot bv) one$, where $eta$ ($xi$) scales traceless (diagonal) part, i.e. shear (bulk) viscosity
- *shocks*: hydro-flows can develop shock waves where density, velocity, temperature, specific entropy jump; typical for hyperbolic PDEs; shocks decelerate, compress and heat up fluid
  - differential form of Euler eqs. breaks down at discontinuity, (weak) integral formulation remains valid; assuming rest frame of shock ($v_s = 0$) and piece-wise constant states left/right leads to Rankine-Hugoniot jump cdts.
    $ rho v = rho' v', quad rho v^2 + P = rho' v'^2 + P', quad (rho e + P) v = (rho' e' + P') v' $
  - presence of shock requires Mach number $cal(M) = v/c_s > 1$, i.e. pre-shock gas streams supersonically into discontinuity, $c_s^2 = (gamma P)/rho$
- *fluid instabilities*: start with two phases $(rho_1, v_1)$, $(rho_2, v_2)$, gravity $vb(g)$ in $-z$-direction, consider single pert. Fourier mode $xi = tilde(xi) e^(i (k x - omega t))$
  - *Rayleigh-Taylor*: $v_1 = v_2 = 0 =>$ disp. rel. $omega^2 = ((rho_2 - rho_1) k g)/(rho_1 + rho_2)$ (phase 1 on top); unstable solutions with $"Im"(omega) > 0$ exist for $rho_1 > rho_2$, i.e. heavy fluid on top of light one
  - *Kelvin-Helmholtz*: $vb(g) = 0$, i.e. pure shear flow $=> omega_(1,2) = k ((rho_1 v_1 + rho_2 v_2))/(rho_1 + rho_2) plus.minus i k sqrt(rho_1 rho_2)/(rho_1 + rho_2) |v_1 - v_2|$; $"Im"(omega) > 0$ always if $v_1 != v_2$, i.e. small perturbation at phase interface will grow rapidly into characteristic KH vortices (fluid turbulences)

= Eulerian hydrodynamics

- important linear and homogeneous PDEs: _Laplace_ $Delta u = 0$, _heat conduction_ $partial_t u = lambda^2 Delta u$, _wave propagation_ $partial_t^2 u = c^2 Delta u$
- classify 2nd-order linear PDEs $a partial_x^2 u + b partial_x partial_y u + c partial_y^2 u + d partial_x u + e partial_y u + f u = g$ according to $D = b^2 - 4 a c$: $D < 0$ elliptic (static problems without time dependence), $D = 0$ parabolic (slowly changing processes, e.g. diffusion), $D > 0$ hyperbolic (dynamical processes that can develop discontinuities)
- *Riemann problem*: IVP for hyperbolic system of two (piece-wise constant) phases characterized at $t = 0$ by state vectors $bU_i = (rho_i, P_i, bv_i)$, $i in {R, L}$, task: evolve system for $t > 0$
  - (always present) _contact wave_: boundary between phases
  - may be sandwiched between _shock_ and/or _rarefaction waves_
  - waves propagate with constant speed, fluid properties $(rho^star, P^star, bv^star)$ at $x = 0$ _constant in time_ for $t > 0$
  - for $bv_L = bv_R = 0$, Riemann problem simplifies to _Sod shock tube_

= Smoothed particle hydrodynamics

- (versatile mesh-free) technique to approximate fluid continuum dynamics through particles (interpolation points)
- advantages: 1. provides automatically adaptive spatial resolution and density 2. ensures conservation of energy, (angular) momentum, mass, entropy 3. Galilean invariant and advection-error-free 4. can deal with complicated geometries and large empty regions due to mesh-free nature 5. robust: does not produce negative $rho$'s and $T$'s
- disadvantage: limited accuracy in multi-dim. flows, gives rise to velocity noise, degrades accuracy and leads to slow convergence

= Finite element methods

- for solving PDEs, good at flexible geometries, odd b.c., allows spatially variable resolution
- central idea: 1. divide domain into smaller regions (_elements_) containing points (_nodes_), possible boundaries: segments (1D), triangles (2D), tetrahedra (3D) 2. choose (linearly indep.) basis fcts. (called _shape fcts._) to describe solution on each element, e.g. polynomial basis 3. $n$ coefficients in element expansion require $n$ nodes to uniquely specify _reconstruction_ inside element

= Monte Carlo Techniques

- lie at the heart of _stochastic simulations_
- *error* of Monte Carlo integration $I_N$ given by width $sigma_N$ of its probability distribution $P_N (I_N)$; always a Gaussian according to *central limit theorem* so $sigma_N = V sqrt((ev(f^2) - ev(f)^2)/N)$
  - $prop 1 \/ sqrt(N)$, irrespective of dimensionality $d$ of integration domain
- *importance sampling*: choose random points preferentially around peaks of function to be integrated
- virtues of *pseudo-random number generators*: repeatability, randomness, speed, portability, long period, insensitivity to seed
- *exact inversion*: method for creating nonuniform PDF $p(y)$ from uniform distr. $q(x) = 1/(b - a)$ over the interval $[a, b]$; uses conservation of probability to equate respective CDFs
  $ Q(x) = integral_(-infinity)^x q(x') dif x' limits(=)^! integral_(-infinity)^y p(y') dif y' = P(y) $
- *rejection method*: used to create uninvertible random number distr. $p(y)$ from distr. $f(y) >= p(y)$ we _can_ produce
  - 1. generate $y$ from $f(y)$ 2. generate $z$ from uniform distr. with bounds $0 <= z < f(y)$ 3. if $z <= p(y)$, return $y$ as sample value 4. else reject trial value for $y$ and start again
- *Markov chain*: discrete sequence of states $x_i$ generated by _Markov process_ $f$ which must 1. preserve eq. distr. $p_"eq" (x)$ of stochastic process 2. starting from $x$, be able to reach any other state $x'$ (_ergodicity_)
- *Metropolis-Hastings algorithm*: 1. from current state $x$, propose new state $x'$ with proposal probability $q(x -> x')$ 2. accept proposed move with probability given by _Hastings ratio_ $r = min(1, (p(x') q(x' -> x))/(p(x) q(x -> x')))$ 3. otherwise reject $x'$ and add $x$ to Markov chain (again)
  - fulfills _detailed balance_: $p_"eq" (x) W_f (x -> x') limits(=)^checkmark p_"eq" (x') W_f (x' -> x)$
